  "notes": "FORMS:\nA compound verb from {上|あ}がる (to go up, to enter a house) + こむ (to go into deeply). In traditional Japanese houses, one steps up from the entrance ({玄関|げんかん}) into the living area, hence \"going up.\"\n\nUSAGE:\nOften implies entering casually, without much ceremony, or even intrusively. More informal than {上|あ}がる alone when used for entering a house.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {家|いえ}に{上|あ}がりこむ (to go into a house)\n- {勝手|かって}に{上|あ}がりこむ (to barge in without permission)",
